/-!
# A surjection with invertible kernel has perfect target

Let `R → A` be a surjective homomorphism of commutative rings whose kernel `I` is an invertible
`R`-module. This file proves that `A` is then a perfect `R`-module, which is the hypothesis of
Stacks 0BZE, and records two consequences: Stacks 0BZE itself holds for such a map, and the
hypotheses are satisfied by the evaluation surjections `B[x] ↠ B`, whose kernel is generated by
the nonzerodivisor `x - b`.

Perfectness is read off the two-term resolution `0 → I → R → A → 0` of
`Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.kernelShortComplex`: an invertible module is finite projective and `R`
is finite free, so both of the outer terms of that sequence are perfect modules, and the third
object of the distinguished triangle a short exact sequence determines in `D(R)` is perfect by
Stacks 066R.

## Main results

* `StacksDraft.Perfect.isPerfectModule_of_finite_projective` — a finite projective module is
  perfect: it is its own one-term resolution.
* `Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.isPerfectModule_of_surjective_of_invertible_ker` — **a surjection
  with invertible kernel has perfect target.**
* `Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.exists_derivedHom_iso_derivedTensor_of_invertible_ker` —
  **Stacks 0BZE for a surjection with invertible kernel**: derived coextension of scalars along
  such a map is a derived tensor product.
* `Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.exists_derivedHom_iso_derivedTensor_factor_class_of_invertible_ker`
  — the tensor factor of Stacks 0BZE has, as an object of `D(A)`, the class of the conormal
  inverse shift `N[-1]` of Stacks 0BZH. This is what
  `Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.nonempty_derivedHom_iso_canonicalDerivedTensor_conormalInverseShift`
  turns into Stacks 0BZH with nothing assumed, at module universe equal to the ring universe; its
  docstring records how.
* `Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.surjective_and_invertible_ker_evalRingHom` — the hypotheses of
  Stacks 0BZH hold at an evaluation surjection `B[x] ↠ B`.
* `Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.isPerfectModule_evalRingHom` — `B` is a perfect `B[x]`-module.

## Implementation notes

The perfectness statement is made at module universe equal to the ring universe, which is the
universe at which `StacksDraft.Perfect.IsPerfect` is used by Stacks 0BZE: the hypothesis of
`Dualizing.exists_derivedHom_iso_derivedTensor` is `IsPerfectModule.{u, u, u + 1}`, and the
hypothesis `hperfect` of `Stacks0BZE.card_of_isIso_comparison` is the same proposition. The
derived category of `ModuleCat.{u} R` carries the standard model there, so the two universe
arguments beyond the ring's are forced.

`Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.kernelShortComplex` is stated with its terms lifted to a module
universe above the ring's, so the conclusion first comes out for `ULift A` and is transported to
`A` along `ULift.moduleEquiv`.

The statements about the evaluation surjection are phrased for an arbitrary `B[x]`-algebra
structure on `B` together with an equation identifying its structure map with
`Polynomial.evalRingHom`, rather than for `(Polynomial.evalRingHom b).toAlgebra`: the surrounding
development quantifies over `[Algebra R A]`, and an algebra instance introduced by `letI` in a
statement is awkward to consume.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BZE](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BZE)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BZH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BZH)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0657](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0657)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 066R](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/066R)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory

-- `StacksFrontier/Homology/DerivedCategory/LocallySmall.lean` installs a locally small model of
-- `D(Mod R)` as a global instance, and the statements imported here are all read at the standard
-- model; the priority settles which of the two a bare `DerivedCategory (ModuleCat R)` means.
attribute [local instance 10000] HasDerivedCategory.standard

universe u v

namespace StacksDraft.Perfect

/-- A finite projective module is a perfect module: the complex consisting of it in degree zero
is already a bounded complex of finite projective modules. -/
theorem isPerfectModule_of_finite_projective {R : Type u} [CommRing R] (M : Type v)
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] [Module.Finite R M] [Module.Projective R M] :
    IsPerfectModule (R := R) M := by
  have key : ∀ i : ℤ,
      Module.Finite R (((CochainComplex.singleFunctor (ModuleCat.{v} R) 0).obj
        (ModuleCat.of R M)).X i) ∧
      Module.Projective R (((CochainComplex.singleFunctor (ModuleCat.{v} R) 0).obj
        (ModuleCat.of R M)).X i) := by
    intro i
    by_cases hi : i = 0
    · subst hi
      let e := (HomologicalComplex.singleObjXSelf (ComplexShape.up ℤ) (0 : ℤ)
        (ModuleCat.of R M)).toLinearEquiv.symm
      exact ⟨Module.Finite.equiv e, Module.Projective.of_equiv e⟩
    · have : Subsingleton (((CochainComplex.singleFunctor (ModuleCat.{v} R) 0).obj
          (ModuleCat.of R M)).X i) :=
        ModuleCat.isZero_iff_subsingleton.mp (HomologicalComplex.isZero_single_obj_X _ _ _ _ hi)
      exact ⟨inferInstance, inferInstance⟩
  exact ⟨(CochainComplex.singleFunctor (ModuleCat.{v} R) 0).obj (ModuleCat.of R M), 0, 0,
    inferInstance, inferInstance, fun i => (key i).1, fun i => (key i).2,
    ⟨(DerivedCategory.singleFunctorIsoCompQ (ModuleCat.{v} R) 0).app _⟩⟩

end StacksDraft.Perfect

namespace Dualizing.InvertibleKernel

/-! ### Perfectness -/

section Perfect

variable {R A : Type u} [CommRing R] [CommRing A] [Algebra R A]

/-- **A surjection with invertible kernel has perfect target.** This is the hypothesis of
Stacks 0BZE, and for such a map it is automatic: the resolution `0 → I → R → A → 0` of
`Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.kernelShortComplex` has an invertible, hence finite projective, kernel
term and a finite free middle term, so both outer objects of the distinguished triangle it
determines in `D(R)` are perfect and Stacks 066R applies to the third. -/
theorem isPerfectModule_of_surjective_of_invertible_ker
    (hf : Function.Surjective (algebraMap R A))
    (hI : Module.Invertible R (RingHom.ker (algebraMap R A))) :
    StacksDraft.Perfect.IsPerfectModule.{u, u, u + 1} (R := R) A := by
  have hS := kernelShortComplex_shortExact.{u, u} (R := R) (A := A) hf
  have h₃ : StacksDraft.Perfect.IsPerfect
      ((DerivedCategory.singleFunctor (ModuleCat.{u} R) 0).obj
        (ModuleCat.of R (ULift.{u} A))) := by
    refine (StacksDraft.Perfect.isPerfect_of_distinguished hS.singleTriangle
      hS.singleTriangle_distinguished).1 ?_ ?_
    · rw [hS.singleTriangle_obj₁]
      have := kernelModule_invertible.{u, u} hI
      exact StacksDraft.Perfect.isPerfectModule_of_finite_projective (R := R)
        (kernelModule.{u, u} R A)
    · rw [hS.singleTriangle_obj₂]
      exact StacksDraft.Perfect.isPerfectModule_of_finite_projective (R := R) (ULift.{u} R)
  exact StacksDraft.Perfect.isPerfect_of_iso
    ((DerivedCategory.singleFunctor (ModuleCat.{u} R) 0).mapIso
      (ULift.moduleEquiv (R := R) (M := A)).symm.toModuleIso) h₃

/-- **Stacks 0BZE for a surjection with invertible kernel.** Derived coextension
of scalars `RHom_R(A, -) : D(R) ⥤ D(A)` along a surjection whose kernel is an invertible
`R`-module is derived tensoring over `R` with a differential graded `(R, A)`-bimodule, whose class
in `D(A)` is the value `RHom_R(A, R)` of that functor at `R`.

The perfectness hypothesis of `Dualizing.exists_derivedHom_iso_derivedTensor` is discharged by
`Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.isPerfectModule_of_surjective_of_invertible_ker`. Note that the shape
of the conclusion is the one Stacks 0BZE is proved in, through a Tate resolution; it is *not* the
derived change-of-rings tensor product of Stacks 06Y6 in which
`Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.derivedHomIsoDerivedTensorConormalInverseShift` states Stacks 0BZH. -/
theorem exists_derivedHom_iso_derivedTensor_of_invertible_ker
    (hf : Function.Surjective (algebraMap R A))
    (hI : Module.Invertible R (RingHom.ker (algebraMap R A))) :
    ∃ M, Nonempty (Dualizing.derivedHom.{u, u, u} (algebraMap R A) ≅
          Dualizing.derivedTensorBimoduleFunctor M) ∧
      Nonempty ((Dualizing.derivedHom.{u, u, u} (algebraMap R A)).obj
          ((DerivedCategory.singleFunctor (ModuleCat.{u} R) 0).obj (ModuleCat.of R R)) ≅
        (DifferentialGradedModule.degreeZeroDerivedEquivalence (R := R) (A := A)).functor.obj
          ((DifferentialGradedModule.toHomotopyCategory
              (DifferentialGradedAlgebra.degreeZero R A) ⋙
            (DifferentialGradedModule.quasiIsomorphisms'
              (DifferentialGradedAlgebra.degreeZero R A)).Q).obj M.rightModule)) :=
  Dualizing.exists_derivedHom_iso_derivedTensor
    (isPerfectModule_of_surjective_of_invertible_ker hf hI)

/-- **The tensor factor of Stacks 0BZE is the conormal inverse shift, as an object of `D(A)`.**
For a surjection with invertible kernel, the differential graded bimodule `M` that Stacks 0BZE
produces from a Tate resolution has, as its class in `D(A)`, exactly the complex `N[-1]` of
Stacks 0BZH: the first conjunct is Stacks 0BZE and the second identifies its graded dual with
`Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.conormalInverseShift`.

The identification comes from the two-term resolution `0 → I → R → A → 0`, through
`Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.derivedHomUnitObjectIsoSingle`, which computes `RHom_R(A, R)` as the
single complex on `N` in degree one; both descriptions of the tensor factor are the value of
`RHom_R(A, -)` at the free module of rank one, so they agree. -/
theorem exists_derivedHom_iso_derivedTensor_factor_class_of_invertible_ker
    (hf : Function.Surjective (algebraMap R A))
    (hI : Module.Invertible R (RingHom.ker (algebraMap R A))) :
    ∃ M, Nonempty (Dualizing.derivedHom.{u, u, u} (algebraMap R A) ≅
          Dualizing.derivedTensorBimoduleFunctor M) ∧
      Nonempty ((DifferentialGradedModule.degreeZeroDerivedEquivalence
            (R := R) (A := A)).functor.obj
          ((DifferentialGradedModule.toHomotopyCategory
              (DifferentialGradedAlgebra.degreeZero R A) ⋙
            (DifferentialGradedModule.quasiIsomorphisms'
              (DifferentialGradedAlgebra.degreeZero R A)).Q).obj M.rightModule) ≅
        DerivedCategory.Q.obj (conormalInverseShift.{u, u} R A)) := by
  obtain ⟨M, ⟨eFun⟩, ⟨eFactor⟩⟩ :=
    exists_derivedHom_iso_derivedTensor_of_invertible_ker hf hI
  refine ⟨M, ⟨eFun⟩, ⟨eFactor.symm ≪≫ ?_⟩⟩
  refine (Dualizing.derivedHom.{u, u, u} (algebraMap R A)).mapIso
      ((DerivedCategory.singleFunctor (ModuleCat.{u} R) 0).mapIso
        (ULift.moduleEquiv (R := R) (M := R)).symm.toModuleIso) ≪≫ ?_
  exact derivedHomUnitObjectIsoSingle.{u, u} hf hI ≪≫
    (conormalInverseShiftIso.{u, u} R A).symm

end Perfect

/-! ### The evaluation surjections of a polynomial algebra in one variable -/

section PolynomialEval

open Polynomial

variable {B : Type u} [CommRing B]

/-- The kernel of an evaluation map `B[x] → B` is an invertible `B[x]`-module: it is the principal
ideal generated by the monic, hence nonzerodivisor, polynomial `x - b`. -/
theorem invertible_ker_evalRingHom (b : B) :
    Module.Invertible B[X] (RingHom.ker (Polynomial.evalRingHom b)) := by
  have hx : ∀ r : B[X], r * (X - C b) = 0 → r = 0 := by
    intro r h
    by_contra hr
    exact (monic_X_sub_C b).mul_right_ne_zero hr (by rw [mul_comm]; exact h)
  exact Module.Invertible.congr
    ((spanSingletonEquiv (X - C b) hx).trans
      (LinearEquiv.ofEq _ _ (Polynomial.ker_evalRingHom b).symm))

/-- **The hypotheses of Stacks 0BZH hold at an evaluation surjection `B[x] ↠ B`.** The map is
surjective because it is the identity on constants, and its kernel is generated by the
nonzerodivisor `x - b`, hence free of rank one and in particular invertible. -/
theorem surjective_and_invertible_ker_evalRingHom [Algebra B[X] B] (b : B)
    (hmap : algebraMap B[X] B = Polynomial.evalRingHom b) :
    Function.Surjective (algebraMap B[X] B) ∧
      Module.Invertible B[X] (RingHom.ker (algebraMap B[X] B)) := by
  rw [hmap]
  exact ⟨fun y => ⟨C y, by simp⟩, invertible_ker_evalRingHom b⟩

/-- **`B` is a perfect `B[x]`-module along an evaluation map.** The kernel of the evaluation map is
free of rank one, so `Dualizing.InvertibleKernel.isPerfectModule_of_surjective_of_invertible_ker`
applies; this discharges the hypothesis of Stacks 0BZE at the evaluation surjections. -/
theorem isPerfectModule_evalRingHom [Algebra B[X] B] (b : B)
    (hmap : algebraMap B[X] B = Polynomial.evalRingHom b) :
    StacksDraft.Perfect.IsPerfectModule.{u, u, u + 1} (R := B[X]) B :=
  isPerfectModule_of_surjective_of_invertible_ker
    (surjective_and_invertible_ker_evalRingHom b hmap).1
    (surjective_and_invertible_ker_evalRingHom b hmap).2

end PolynomialEval

end Dualizing.InvertibleKernel

